<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">French protectorate in Morocco</h1><p class="st-meta">1912 CE – 1956 CE · capital Rabat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">French rule over most of Morocco from 1912, shaped by Lyautey's modern cities and ended by a nationalist movement led by the sultan himself.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir-crisis">Agadir crisis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-hafid-of-morocco">Abd al-Hafid</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/alaouite-sultanate">Alaouite Sultanate</a> to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fez">Treaty of Fez</a> in March 1912, establishing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-lyautey">Hubert Lyautey</a>, the first resident-general, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> the capital and spent years subduing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> tribes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan and makhzen remained in place, but the resident-general governed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-lyautey">Lyautey</a> built new European cities beside the old medinas and developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casablanca">Casablanca's</a> port. French settlers took fertile land, and phosphate mining grew. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-dahir">Berber Dahir</a> of 1930 sparked nationalist protest, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istiqlal-party">Istiqlal</a> party demanded independence in 1944.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> deposed and exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-v-of-morocco">Sultan Mohammed</a> V in 1953, provoking violent unrest. He returned in 1955, and France recognised Moroccan independence in March 1956. Mohammed V adopted the title of king, forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-protectorate-in-morocco.jpg" alt="French protectorate in Morocco" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Flaspec · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Merchant_flag_of_French_Morocco.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1912 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rabat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hubert Lyautey, Mohammed V</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Morocco</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Morocco, Protectorate of Morocco</dd></dl></aside></div>
